/**
\defgroup faa_di_bruno Faà di Bruno's formula and partial Bell polynomials
\brief Faà di Bruno's formula for the \f$N\f$-th derivative of a composite function \f$f(g(x))\f$ and the partial Bell polynomials \f$B_{n,k}\f$ up to order 7.

Include `<laplace/math/faa_di_bruno.hpp>` and call functions in this module as `laplace::math::NAME`. It provides \ref laplace::math::bell_partial "bell_partial()" for partial Bell polynomials \f$B_{n,k}\f$ with \f$1 \le k \le n \le 7\f$, and \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d1 "chain_rule_d1()" through \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d7 "chain_rule_d7()" for composite derivatives up to order 7. All functions are marked `LAPLACE_HD` for host and Kokkos device execution. Measured maximum errors stay within 5.03 ulp of the absolute term sum on gcc and CUDA, bounded at 1.0 to 7.5 ulp in automated tests.

## Mathematical definitions

The module evaluates partial Bell polynomials and univariate composite derivatives.

For integers \f$1 \le k \le n \le 7\f$, the partial Bell polynomial \f$B_{n,k}(g_1, \dots, g_{n-k+1})\f$ of degree \f$n\f$ and order \f$k\f$ is defined by
\f[
B_{n,k}(g_1, \dots, g_{n-k+1}) = \sum \frac{n!}{j_1! j_2! \cdots j_{n-k+1}!} \prod_{m=1}^{n-k+1} \left( \frac{g_m}{m!} \right)^{j_m},
\f]
where the sum runs over all non-negative integer partition indices \f$j_m \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}\f$ satisfying \f$\sum_{m=1}^{n-k+1} j_m = k\f$ and \f$\sum_{m=1}^{n-k+1} m j_m = n\f$. Here \f$g_m \in \mathbb{R}\f$ is the argument of order \f$m \in \{1, \dots, n - k + 1\}\f$, representing \f$g^{(m)}(x)\f$. Outside \f$1 \le k \le n \le 7\f$, the polynomial is \f$0\f$. Special evaluations yield the Stirling numbers of the second kind \f$S(n, k)\f$ and a binomial identity:
\f[
B_{n,k}(1, 1, \dots, 1) = S(n, k), \qquad B_{n,k}(1, 2, \dots, n - k + 1) = \binom{n}{k} k^{n-k}.
\f]

For sufficiently differentiable functions \f$f\f$ and \f$g\f$, Faà di Bruno's formula gives the \f$N\f$-th derivative of \f$f(g(x))\f$ at \f$x \in \mathbb{R}\f$ for \f$N \in \{1, \dots, 7\}\f$:
\f[
\frac{\mathrm{d}^N}{\mathrm{d}x^N} f(g(x)) = \sum_{k=1}^N f_k \, B_{N,k}(g_1, \dots, g_{N-k+1}).
\f]
Here \f$f_k = f^{(k)}(g(x)) \in \mathbb{R}\f$ and \f$g_m = g^{(m)}(x) \in \mathbb{R}\f$. Each order's expanded polynomial appears in its function entry. For bivariate compositions, see \ref chain_pair "the bivariate chain rule".

## Domain and conventions

All derivative arrays are 1-indexed by derivative order, and element `0` is never read. For \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d1 "chain_rule_d1()" through \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d7 "chain_rule_d7()", arrays `f` and `g` must each hold at least \f$N + 1\f$ doubles, storing \f$f^{(k)}(g(x))\f$ in `f[k]` and \f$g^{(m)}(x)\f$ in `g[m]` for \f$k, m = 1, \dots, N\f$.

For \ref laplace::math::bell_partial "bell_partial()", valid integer parameters satisfy \f$1 \le k \le n \le 7\f$. Within that range, array `g` must hold at least \f$n - k + 2\f$ doubles, storing \f$g_m\f$ in `g[m]` for \f$m = 1, \dots, n - k + 1\f$. Outside \f$1 \le k \le n \le 7\f$, \ref laplace::math::bell_partial "bell_partial()" returns \f$0.0\f$ without reading `g`.

If any required input in `f` or `g` is NaN, the called function returns quiet NaN.

## Numerical methods

The module evaluates explicit monomial expressions instead of searching partitions at runtime. The 28 partial Bell polynomials \f$B_{n,k}\f$ for \f$1 \le k \le n \le 7\f$ are written out as sums of monomials with positive integer coefficients using SymPy's `bell(n, k, ...)` in SymPy 1.14.0 (Meurer et al. 2017).

For \f$N = 1\f$, \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d1 "chain_rule_d1()" evaluates \f$f_1 g_1\f$ directly. For \f$N \ge 2\f$, \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d2 "chain_rule_d2()" through \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d7 "chain_rule_d7()" sum \f$f_k B_{N,k}\f$ from \f$k = N\f$ down to \f$k = 1\f$.

## Measured accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for error definitions, reference generation, and test bounds. Reference values in `tests/data/reference/faa_di_bruno.csv` come from `tools/reference/faa_di_bruno.py` across 1950 cases combining random derivatives and practical compositions.

Because terms can alternate in sign, catastrophic cancellation occurs when composite derivatives vanish or nearly vanish. For example, \f$\log(\exp(x)) = x\f$ has zero derivatives for \f$N \ge 2\f$, so plain relative errors reach \f$10^{16}\f$ to \f$10^{18}\f$ ulps from input rounding. Accuracy is therefore measured in ulps of the absolute term sum:
\f[
S = \sum_{k=1}^N |f_k| B_{N,k}(|g_1|, \dots, |g_{N-k+1}|).
\f]
The measured maximum errors on gcc and CUDA alongside the test bounds in `tests/math/test_faa_di_bruno.cpp` are shown below.

| Function | Order \f$N\f$ | Measured max error gcc (ulp of \f$S\f$) | Measured max error CUDA (ulp of \f$S\f$) | Test bound (ulp of \f$S\f$) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d1 "chain_rule_d1()" | 1 | 0.50 | 0.50 | 1.0 |
| \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d2 "chain_rule_d2()" | 2 | 1.73 | 1.73 | 3.0 |
| \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d3 "chain_rule_d3()" | 3 | 2.21 | 2.21 | 3.5 |
| \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d4 "chain_rule_d4()" | 4 | 3.08 | 3.03 | 4.5 |
| \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d5 "chain_rule_d5()" | 5 | 3.05 | 3.05 | 4.5 |
| \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d6 "chain_rule_d6()" | 6 | 3.96 | 3.96 | 6.0 |
| \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d7 "chain_rule_d7()" | 7 | 5.03 | 5.03 | 7.5 |

Automated tests also verify \ref laplace::math::bell_partial "bell_partial()" with exact equality against the Stirling and binomial identities for \f$1 \le k \le n \le 7\f$, and check zero return outside that range. See `docs/numerics.md` for dataset details and full measurements.

## Speed

Measured with 16 inputs read from memory, calls take 2.8–6.6 ns on one gcc thread, 1.7–1.9 ns on 16 OpenMP threads, and 0.31 ns on CUDA.

## Example

The following example evaluates the first two composite derivatives of \f$f(g(x))\f$ inside a `Kokkos::parallel_for` kernel.

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/math/faa_di_bruno.hpp>
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>

void compute_derivatives(const Kokkos::View<const double**>& f,
                         const Kokkos::View<const double**>& g,
                         Kokkos::View<double*>& d1,
                         Kokkos::View<double*>& d2,
                         int count) {
  Kokkos::parallel_for("eval_faa_di_bruno", count, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    // f(i, k) holds f^(k) at g(x_i) for k = 1..2
    // g(i, j) holds g^(j) at x_i for j = 1..2
    const double f_row[3] = {0.0, f(i, 1), f(i, 2)};
    const double g_row[3] = {0.0, g(i, 1), g(i, 2)};
    d1(i) = laplace::math::chain_rule_d1(f_row, g_row);
    d2(i) = laplace::math::chain_rule_d2(f_row, g_row);
  });
}
\endcode

## Module functions

Functions in this module are called as `laplace::math::NAME`.

- \ref laplace::math::bell_partial "bell_partial()" — Partial Bell polynomial \f$B_{n,k}(g_1, \dots, g_{n-k+1})\f$ for \f$1 \le k \le n \le 7\f$
- \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d1 "chain_rule_d1()" — First composite derivative \f$\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x} f(g(x))\f$
- \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d2 "chain_rule_d2()" — Second composite derivative \f$\frac{\mathrm{d}^2}{\mathrm{d}x^2} f(g(x))\f$
- \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d3 "chain_rule_d3()" — Third composite derivative \f$\frac{\mathrm{d}^3}{\mathrm{d}x^3} f(g(x))\f$
- \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d4 "chain_rule_d4()" — Fourth composite derivative \f$\frac{\mathrm{d}^4}{\mathrm{d}x^4} f(g(x))\f$
- \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d5 "chain_rule_d5()" — Fifth composite derivative \f$\frac{\mathrm{d}^5}{\mathrm{d}x^5} f(g(x))\f$
- \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d6 "chain_rule_d6()" — Sixth composite derivative \f$\frac{\mathrm{d}^6}{\mathrm{d}x^6} f(g(x))\f$
- \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_d7 "chain_rule_d7()" — Seventh composite derivative \f$\frac{\mathrm{d}^7}{\mathrm{d}x^7} f(g(x))\f$

## References

- A. Meurer and others (2017), SymPy: symbolic computing in Python, PeerJ Computer Science 3:e103
*/
